this is relatively confusing when taken in concert with 663 through 670. whats a valueLength vs a length?

maybe:
```
const size_t length = isByteLengthEncoding ? tagInfo & 0xE : 1 << (tagInfo & 0xE);
```

or if you prefer:
```
const size_t length = [isByteLengthEncoding](uint8_t bytes) {
    return isByteLengthEncoding ? bytes : 1 << bytes;
}(tagInfo & 0xE); 
```

or,
```
struct TagLength
{
   uint8_t tag;
   uint8_t isbyteLengthEncoded : 1;
   uint8_t reserved : 3;
   uint8_t length : 3;
   uint8_t isValid : 1;
}; // pack incantation here and if you're paranoid a static assert on its size

TagLength tl;
std::memcpy(&tl, &buf[/* offset stuff */], sizeof(tl));
const size_t length = tl.isByteLengthEncoded ? tl.length : 1 << tl.length;
```

or,
```
// struct stuff from above
size_t length = tl.length;
if (!tl.isByteLengthEncoded)
{
    length = 1 << length;
}
```

also should this function really be hosting this logic? don't we have a bunch of instances of this tlv data structure throughout the spec?
